<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Diet of Worms</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1521">1521</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Diet of Worms (1521)</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Diet of Worms was the imperial assembly held in 1521 by the young Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> at Worms. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a> appeared before it and refused to recant his writings. The resulting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edict-of-worms">Edict of Worms</a> declared him an outlaw and banned his works, but it was poorly enforced, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> spread in the following years.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/diet-of-worms.jpg" alt="Diet of Worms" width="320" height="166" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anton von Werner · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%9B%D1%8E%D1%82%D0%B5%D1%80_%D0%B2_%D0%92%D0%BE%D1%80%D0%BC%D1%81%D0%B5.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In June 1520 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-leo-x">Pope Leo X</a> issued the bull Exsurge Domine, which listed 41 purported errors in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a>'s Ninety-five Theses and other writings. Luther was excommunicated in January 1521 and summoned by the emperor to the diet at Worms. Frederick III, Elector of Saxony, obtained a promise of safe passage, which was essential after the treatment of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jan-hus">Jan Hus</a>, tried and executed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-constance">Council of Constance</a> in 1415 despite a promise of safe conduct. The diet sat from January to 25 May 1521 at the Bischofshof palace, with Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> presiding.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Luther reached Worms on 16 April. On 17 April the spokesman Johann von Eck asked whether a collection of 25 titles was his and whether he would revoke them. Luther asked for time and was given until 4 p.m. the next day.</p><p>On 18 April he said the books were his but not all of one sort: some had been well received even by his enemies, some attacked abuses of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a>, and some were harsh attacks on individuals. He would not reject the first two kinds and, if shown by Scripture that he was wrong, he would reject the third. He concluded that unless convinced by Scripture or clear reason he could not and would not recant, since it was neither safe nor right to go against conscience. According to tradition he added, Here I stand, I cannot do otherwise.</p><p>He was not arrested. Given safe conduct, he left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wittenberg">Wittenberg</a>, but Frederick III, fearing for his safety, had men stage a highway attack and take him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wartburg">Wartburg Castle</a>, where he stayed disguised as a knight from early May 1521 to early March 1522. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edict-of-worms">Edict of Worms</a>, issued on 25 May 1521, declared him a heretic and an enemy of the state and permitted anyone to kill him without legal consequence. The diet also let <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> reform the administration of his lands, appointing his brother Ferdinand as Archduke and Imperial Lieutenant.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The Edict was never enforced in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, because of rising support for Luther among the people and the protection of certain princes. In the Habsburg Netherlands, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>'s direct rule and his regent Margaret of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, it was enforced against Luther's active supporters. Jacob Proost, prior of the Augustinian monastery in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a>, was arrested in December 1521 and made to recant in February 1522, and the monks Jan van <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/essen">Essen</a> and Hendrik Vos were burned at the stake in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a> on 1 July 1523. The Diets of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a> of 1522 and 1524 failed to carry out the Edict. Historians such as Carter Lindberg see Luther's stand as crystallising the principle of the authority of Scripture over ecclesiastical hierarchy.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Diet of Worms” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Frederick the Wise, who had founded the university where Luther taught, refused to hand him over after the <b>Diet of Worms</b> in 1521 and hid him in the Wartburg, where Luther translated the New Testament.</blockquote><a href="/empire/electorate-of-saxony">Electorate of Saxony · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Excommunicated in January 1521, he appeared that April before the emperor Charles V at the <b>Diet of Worms</b> and refused to recant unless proved wrong by scripture and plain reason.</blockquote><a href="/movement/protestant-reformation">Protestant Reformation · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>The bishops lost control of Worms itself, which became a free imperial city; the Concordat of Worms in 1122 and Luther's appearance at the <b>Diet of Worms</b> in 1521 took place there.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-worms">Prince-Bishopric of Worms</a></li></ul></section>
